Thus, AX is at right-angles to each of BX and XK. And since AB is equal to AK, the (square) on AB is also equal to the (square) on AK. And the (sum of the squares) on AX and XB is equal to the (square) on AB. For the angle at X (is) a right-angle [Prop. 1.47]. And the (sum of the squares) on AX and XK is equal to the (square) on AK [Prop. 1.47]. Thus, the (sum of the squares) on AX and XB is equal to the (sum of the squares) on AX and XK. Let the (square) on AX have been subtracted from both. Thus, the remaining (square) on BX is equal to the remaining (square) on XK. Thus, BX (is) equal to XK. So, similarly, we can show that the straight-lines joined from X to P and S are equal to each of BX and XK.


